From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: let the periodic report move a client
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

A roamed client could not be corrected by the one message that says where
clients ARE.

The Associated Clients TLV of a Topology Response is every node's periodic
statement of the clients it is serving. When a client appeared there under a
node other than the one the controller had it filed under, the report was
discarded as a "conflicting STA-association from multiple Agents". Roaming was
left entirely to the association EVENT, which does handle it - it removes the
client from the old node and adds it to the new one.

Events get lost. This mesh has measured CMDUs going nowhere at startup, and a
controller restart misses every event that fires while it is down. Once an
event is missed the client is filed under the wrong node forever, because the
periodic report - the only thing that could notice - is forbidden from saying
so.

Measured 2026-09-06 on the lab mesh, and the chain is complete:

  the kitchen node HAS aa:9f:01:68:1b:d9   wlan1 5 GHz, -46 dBm, in its
                                           own map.agent model
  the kitchen node REPORTS it              node heard 0 s ago
  the controller DISCARDS the report       filed under the corridor node
  -> 51 minutes and counting, with nothing in the system able to end it

The discard was protecting against something real: during a roam both the old
and the new node list the client, and letting either one win would flap. So
the tie is broken instead of ignored, using a number the TLV already carries -
conn_time, seconds since the client associated to the reporting node.

The comparison has to be between absolute instants. A stored conn_time ages
with the wall clock while the number itself sits still, so comparing it
against a freshly reported one asks which was smaller when it was written,
which is not the question. The first cut of this patch did exactly that and
was measured failing: 16:79:d1:37:ae:0b had been on the bedroom node for 60 s,
the stored value said 5 s and had been sitting there for 87 s - really 92 -
and 60 >= 5 refused the move that 60 < 92 demanded. So the instant of
association, time(NULL) - conntime, is what is compared, against s->assoc_time
which already holds it. Later wins; equal or earlier is dropped as before.

The same-node branch now also writes the BSSID and the state. A steer between
bands inside one box changes the BSSID, and steering, assoc_control and the
metrics queries are all addressed by BSSID - a stale one aims them at a BSS
the client has left. And a node listing a client among its associated clients
is saying that client is associated: not writing the state is why
b8:09:8a:50:f2:2a, associated to the controller's own radio and carrying
traffic, sat in the database with associated=0.

